 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "me-telo-percussi",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "me telo meo percussi",
   "trans": "I struck myself with my own weapon",
   "body": "The god of love confesses he is a victim of love: <em>me telo meo percussi</em> — \"I struck myself with my own dart\". The famous archer (<em>praeclarus sagittarius</em>) has, ironically, shot himself; the emphatic <em>me ... meo</em> drives the point home.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The irony of <em>me telo meo percussi</em> is delicious: the great archer Cupid has wounded himself with his own arrow, the juxtaposed <em>me ... meo</em> stressing that the god of love has fallen in love against his own interest.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "bestia-caput",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Tragic Irony",
   "quote": "bestia ... viderer et ferro caput excideres meum",
   "trans": "I might seem a beast, and you might cut off my head with a knife",
   "body": "Cupid spells out what the sisters’ plot would have meant: he would \"seem a <em>bestia</em>\" and Psyche would cut off the very head whose eyes she loves. The reader recalls Chapter 22, where she really did raise the blade — disaster only just averted.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The tragic irony of <em>bestia ... ferro caput excideres meum</em> recalls Psyche’s actual attempt: she nearly beheaded the ‘beast’ who was her divine husband, making Cupid’s reproach cut close to the bone.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "haec-anaphora",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "haec ... cavenda censebam, haec ... remonebam",
   "trans": "I urged you to guard against these things ... I warned you of these things",
   "body": "The repeated, fronted <em>haec</em> (\"these very things\") in two balanced clauses hammers home how often Cupid had warned her. The anaphora makes his exasperation audible — he told her, again and again.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The anaphora of <em>haec ... haec</em> emphasises the repeated warnings Cupid gave; the balanced clauses and adverbs <em>identidem ... semper</em> stress that Psyche’s betrayal came despite his constant care.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "consiliatrices-egregiae",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Sarcasm",
   "quote": "illae ... consiliatrices egregiae tuae",
   "trans": "those marvellous counsellors of yours",
   "body": "<em>egregiae consiliatrices</em> (\"marvellous advisers\") is heavy sarcasm for the wicked sisters, whose <em>perniciosum magisterium</em> (\"ruinous instruction\") destroyed Psyche’s happiness. The mock-praise drips with contempt.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The sarcasm of <em>consiliatrices egregiae</em> for the murderous sisters, set against <em>perniciosi magisterii</em>, conveys Cupid’s bitter contempt and foreshadows the revenge he promises on them.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "fuga-punivero",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "te vero tantum fuga mea punivero",
   "trans": "you, however, I shall punish only with my flight",
   "body": "Cupid’s punishment of Psyche is gentle to the point of heartbreak: <em>tantum fuga mea</em> — \"only with my flight\". The harshest thing the god of love can do to her is to leave; the loss itself is the penalty.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The pathos of <em>te ... tantum fuga mea punivero</em> lies in its restraint: the only punishment Cupid inflicts on Psyche is to leave her, his departure (and her loss) being penalty enough.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "proripuit",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Vivid Action",
   "quote": "pinnis in altum se proripuit",
   "trans": "on his wings he hurled himself into the heights",
   "body": "The chapter ends on a single violent movement: <em>se proripuit</em> (\"he tore himself away/upward\") on his wings into the <em>altum</em>. The forceful verb leaves Psyche — and the reader — abruptly abandoned, gazing after him.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The energetic <em>pinnis in altum se proripuit</em> ends the speech with abrupt, violent motion: Cupid’s sudden upward flight enacts his departure and leaves Psyche stranded, setting up her despair in the next chapter.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-01",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "praeclarus ille sagittarius ipse",
   "trans": "that famous archer himself",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Cupid describes himself in grand, almost boastful terms. This makes his confession that he wounded himself all the more striking.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-02",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "percussi te -que coniugem meam feci",
   "trans": "I struck myself and made you my wife",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two verbs are joined by -que: the wound leads straight to the marriage. Love, for Cupid as for anyone, was something that happened to him.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Sarcasm",
   "quote": "scilicet",
   "trans": "doubtless",
   "line": "",
   "body": "\"Doubtless\" is bitterly sarcastic: Cupid suggests that the reward for his love was to be thought a beast and to have his head cut off.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-04",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Hyperbaton",
   "quote": "caput excideres meum",
   "trans": "you might cut off my head",
   "line": "",
   "body": "caput and meum are separated, so \"my\" comes last. The reader is left with the shock that the head in question is the god's own.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Paradox",
   "quote": "istos amatores tuos oculos",
   "trans": "those eyes, your lovers",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Cupid calls his eyes Psyche's lovers. It is a tender compliment and a reproach at once: she wanted to destroy the very eyes that adored her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-06",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "benivole",
   "trans": "kindly",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Cupid insists that his warnings were meant kindly, which makes Psyche's disobedience seem worse and his hurt clearer.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-07",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Gerundive",
   "quote": "cavenda",
   "trans": "things to be guarded against",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The gerundive expresses obligation: these were dangers Psyche had to avoid. It shows that the rules were clear and she broke them.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-08",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Foreshadowing",
   "quote": "dabunt actutum mihi poenas",
   "trans": "will soon pay me the penalty",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The threat against the sisters is fulfilled in sections 11 and 12. The old-fashioned word actutum (\"at once\") gives it a solemn, final ring.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-09",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "illae … te vero",
   "trans": "they … but you",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The sisters and Psyche are set against each other: they will be punished harshly, she only by his absence.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
